SentinelOne published the first public analysis of fast16 in April 2026 and dated its oldest components to around 2005, about two years before Stuxnet became active; no source places that disclosure at LABScon or describes earlier speculation about centrifuges. Broadcom's analysis appeared on 2026-05-16 and still calls fast16 pre-Stuxnet, while Kim Zetter argues it was contemporaneous with Stuxnet if it targeted Iran. LS-DYNA and AUTODYN are general explicit-dynamics simulation programs that are also used for explosive simulation.
